Abbreviations:
	:abbreviate --c <CR>---cut---cut---cut---
		when this text typed replace with that
		<CR> carriage return so it'll start on new line

	:ab --c <CR>---cut---cut---cut---<C-O>:center<CR><DOWN>
		<C-O> 'a control Oh' while in insert mode this
			takes into command for only one command
		The one and only command is :center then automatically
			goes back to insert mode
		<CR> needed after that tcommand for execution 
		normal execution of meta character <DOWN>

	Left hand side of abbreviate (first argument to ab)
		1. any alpha numeric combo
		2. non alpha numeric combo followed by an alpha numeric
			ex: ab #1 I'll make that my top priority
		3. final character has to 
	
	Expanding Abbreviations:
		 when using ab, if the ab identifer typed then followed by a 
		 	non alpha numeric like back tick ` or percent % it will
			still expand 

	:ab
		shows the current Abbreviations

	Abbreviations happend in command mode command line too!

	iabbr insert mode abbreviation
	cabbr command mode abbreviation 

	Computed Abbreviations:
		:iabbr <expr> TS strftime("%c")
			<expr> marker/flag indicates that the last argument is not
				a simple replacement but an expression in vim script
	
		:iabbr <expr> PPP getreg('')
			get the contents of this (nameless/first) register


Maps:
	kinda like abbreviations but dont require an extra character to be typed 
		afterwards like a space,<CR>, or any non alpha numeric 

	like a macro. as soon as the last character is typed it is executed
		even if the map trigger/key is within a word. 

	maps are available for ALL modes

	insertion map:
		imap similar to iabbr

	vmaps visual mode maps

	Unremappable Maps
		calling another mapping within another

		Vi will detect infinite recursion of mappings and wont execute
			anything

		Soultion: nnoremap
			:nnoremap v <C-V>
			:nnoremap <C-V> v
			if the above were nmap then we enter infinite loop and nothing
				will happen. nnoremap makes so the mapping wont call
				another mapping, hence 'no re-map'

Vim Sripting or Vimle
	source % run current file from
	Imperative dynamic language
	Each statement is terminated by a newline
	based around statements

	continuation charater is the backlash \ on the next line
		let full_name = 
		\  first_name . ' ' . middle.intial . ' ' . family_name

	vertical bars or pipes for multiple statements on same line

	every command on the vim command line after a colon is a valid
		vim script command

	4 built in types:
		String
		Number
		List
		Dictionary

		String:
			inside single quoed strings backslahes are not escape characters
			two single quotes within a within a single quoted string will get
				you a single quote

		Number:
			integer, scientific e notation, octal , hex

		List: 
			[1,2,3]
			can be non- homogeneous types within the same list
			nesting allowed

		Dictionary:
			{ 'a':1, 'b':2, 'c': '3PO' }
			like python dict
			if key repeated then value over written

	:help expr9 
		for type help

	Comments:
		" this is a comment
		echo "normal string print" | "with a comment on same line

	Variables:
		Need initial key word let Before each variable declaration
		Vim Script is dynamically typed. Once a variable is given a value of a
			specific type when changing values the values must stay within 
			that type otherwise TypeError
			UNLESS unlet keyword is used to remove the previous instantiation

		Defining Variable with Specific scope:
			g:varname 	Glboal
			s:			local to script file
			w:			local to current window
			t: 			local to current tab page
			b:								buffer
			l:								function
			a:			function argument
			v:			vim predefined variable

			when specific scope not specified then the surroundings define scope

			Each scope name (b:, w:, g:) is symbol table for that type which is
				a dictionary

				g:['varname'] == g:varname
			
			can manipulate the entire variable namespace
			" Delete all the variables belonging to the current buffer
			for varname in keys(b:)
				unlet b:[varname]
			endfor

		Pseudo-Variables:
			&varname akin to setting a vim option
				example: let &tabstop = 4 
			&l:varname set local vim option
			&g:varname set global vim option
			@varname vim register
				example: let @a = val
					give reg a this val
			$varname environment variable
				example: echo $HOME

Control Structures:
	for varname in list
		statements()
	endfor

	"parallel iteration. 3 elements per list
	for [var1, var2, var3] in list_of_lists
		statements()
	endfor

	keywords break and continue are available 

Built-in functions:
	:help function-list

	String functions:
		let len = strlen(str)

		let substring = strpart(str,start_index,length)
			of string str start at index position start_index and length much

		let tail = strpart(str, start_index)
			get substring til end

		let ss = str[start : end] "inclusive slice
		let tail = str[start:]
		let head = str[:end]
		!!! let single_char = str[-1] !!!! no matter what will return empty str
		single character gets can NOT be negative! 
		negative slices only work in ranges (with colon)
			work-around:
				let single_char = str[-1:-1]

	let formatted_text = printf("%-2s %d", text num)
		does not print to standard output just returns string to variable

	let lower_case = tolower(txt)
	let upper_case = toupper(txt)

	let tagged = tr(markup, "{}", "<>")
		in the string markup replace opening curly bracket with opening arrow
			bracket, the replace(substitute) closing curly with closing arrow
		character by charcter, this for that substitution 

	let line = repeat('-', 78)
		creates a string with 78 concatenated '-'

	let path_to_this_file = expand('%')

	let path_to_prev_file = expand('#)
		full path tp previous editted file

	let curse_word = expand('<cword>')
		grab the word the cursor is over

	let curse_file = expand('<cfile>')
		detects for slash separated words (a path)

	let config_path = expand('~/.vimrc')
		configures full path

PAttern MAtching:
	let matches = str ~= 'pattern'
		matches value will be true if str matches 'pattern'
	
	let match_index = match(str, 'pattern', start_index, n)
		match_index will be where in the str it matches
		if it does find the pattern it returns -1 b/c 0
			is a valid index where it could be found
		if start_index is specifeid then the match will start that many 
			characters into the str
		if n is specified then we are not asking for the first it matches.
			we return where it matches for the nth time
		
	let match_to = matchend(str, 'pattern', start_index, n)
		returns the index at the end of the match.

	let matched_str = matchstr(str, 'pattern')
		returns what sub-string matched str

	let submatches = matchlist(str, 'p\(at\)t\(er\)n')
		returns a list of each capturing 

	let first_substitute = substitute(txt, pattern, replacement, '')
		if pattern matches in txt change it out wiht replacement once
		this does not change the orignal variable txt but returns a new
			copy of this substituted string in first_substitute

	let all_sanitizae = substitute(txt, pattern, replacement, 'g')
		'g' for global. replace all matches of pattern in txt with
			replacement
		this does not change the orignal variable txt but returns a new
			copy of this substituted string in first_substitute

Input Output:
	echo can take one or more strings
		echo "this way" "ok more"
	echo can also take a general expression and whatever that expression 
		returns echo will print it
	echoerr is the same as an error message that vim will generate and goes 
		the error log that keeps each session
	the error log can be accessed thru :mes
	echo and echoerr are not functions they are commands

	let name = input("Enter your name: ")
		<enter> concludes the input

------------------------------
	let x = input("name here: ")

	if confirm('your name is really '.x.'?', "&Yes\n&No", 1)
		let choices = ['shall i call you: ',
						\ ' 1: '.x,
						\ ' 2: Bruce'
						\]
		let nm = inputlist(choices)

		echo "\n\n chose:" nm
		" could be choices[nm] 
	endif
------------------------------
	confirm(a,b,c)
		a -> prompt to ask for input with
		b -> options to display. The character after the ampersand becomes 
			a valid response option. The default option will be square braketted
			and the other(s) will be parenthesis around the first character
		c -> default option
		return value -> the ordinal position (starts at 1) of the display
			chosen
			vim treat zero as false so the body of the 'if' will always be 
				entered

		see confirm.vim

	inputlist(lst)
		displays: the lst separated by newlines then the prompt below
		"Type number and <Enter> or click with the mouse (q or empty cancels):"
		returns the index position 

Buffer(file) interactions:
	echo getline(10)
		prints the 10th line of file to the message area

	echo getline(2,5)
		gets the 2nd line to the fifth line inclusive
		special arguments: 
			'.' means whatever line the cursor is on
			'$' means the end of the file
		returns a list of strings

	let curline = line('.')
		returns the line number where cursor is on
	example usage:
		let context = getline(curline-2,curline+2)
		
 	setline('.', new_txt)
		replace the line where the cursor is with the text in new_txt
		if new_txt was a list of strings then starting at '.' each string
			will over write as many lines as there are elements in the list

	call append('$', '__END__')
		insert/add __END__ at the end of this file

	let she_bang = append(0,'#!/usr/local/bin/python')
		the new first line of this buffer
		push the existing content down

	usage example:
		:nmap ;c :call append(line('.')-1, '//===[ Comment ]===')<CR>
		
Cursor Control Functions:
	col('.')
		which column the cursor is on
		other special strings than '.' find at :help col

	virtcol('.')
		virtual column number

   	virtual column number VERSUS column number
		regarding the line immediately above this, since i used 
		a tab then typed a 'v' the column number of that 'v' would 
		be 2. The virtual column number would consider how spaces 
		in the tab so the virtual column number of the 'v' is 4.

	win() and wincol()
		the relative number base off the currnt window size

	call cursor(line, col)
		move the cursor to the line and column of said arguments

	call search('^\s*#')
		moving the cursor using pattern matching
		cursor will to next match
		this pattern will move the cursor to the next comment line (#)


	call search('^\s*#', 'b')
		search for this patteern going backwards 'b'


	let line_num = call search('^\s*#', 'n')
		search with NO movement but return the line number of found pattern

***In Vim Script***
	it is an error not to consider the return value of a function.
	above with search(), when we dont care about the return value we MUST
	use 'call' and otherwise use 'let var_name'

	let save_cursor = getpos('.')
		return a list of 4 pieces of information. 
		returns alist of integers
			[ which_buffer, line_num, col_num, virtual_offset]
				which_buffer -> current buffer is 0
				virtual_offset -> regards tabs as 1 space/column. 

	cal setpos('.', save_cursor)
		'.' means setting the position of the cursor
		save_cursor is a list with 4 elements

System and File System functions:
	let file_paths = glob('~/scr/**/*README*')
		returns a list of strings of paths with the matches
		matches with files in the home directory(~) that are in the scr
			directory, then wild card (**) so any directory in scr
			that contain a file with 'README' in the file name
	
	let file_paths = globpath('.,~,/usr/local/scr', '**/*README*')
		'.,~,/usr/local/scr' a string that is a comma separated list
			of paths of where to start looking for the glob match specifed
			in the second argument 

		returns a list of strings that are paths

	let file_name = 'sub_dir/file.txt'
	let important_bit = fnamemodify(file_name, modify_how)
		modify_how is a special string options are in modify_how.png
		:help filename-modifers

	let cwd = getcwd()
	execute 'chdir '.new_dir
		'chdir' is a command not a function so the 'execute' is necessary

	call mkdir(new_dir_path)
		makes new folder. can take an absolute path but all folders in between
			have to exist if not then error
			"build at the end"
	
	call mkdir(new_dir_path, 'p')
		make new folder and create any new intermediary folders
		"build down"

	let failed = rename(old_name, new_name)
		file name can be a path
		return possible error message

	let failed = delete(file_name)
		can take a path

Read and write to a file:
	let line = readfile(file_name)
		return a list of strings. one line per string

	let failed = writefile(line, file_name)
		
	let unique_words = system('uniq', words)
		second argument is piped to the command's STDIN
		return STDOUT to var unique_words
		if you did not care about the STDOUT the use call system('uniq', words)

	let html_source = system('curl -f'.'url)
		fork system command and return its STDOUT

Code Execute functions:
	"create code out of string"
	'call' executes the following command/function and neutralizes the return
		value
	
	'execute' or 'exec' will convert the following string to executable code
	let cmd = '.-'.context_lines.'delete '.(2*context_lines +1)
	exec cmd

	let exx = input('type a Vim Expression: ')   "strftime("%c") is enter
	echo eval(exx) 		"will print the time/date format
